{
  "dom_id": "step-21128",
  "name": "Example: \n                    Identifying the Graph of the Square Root Function",
  "problem": "Which of the following could be the graph of $y = \\sqrt{x}$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14220666/assets/7646aec895e67700f9ddaaa52dabb4d01edb12165add5fbc1a5b464df1a26e52.png)",
  "worked_solution": "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\sqrt x{:}$\n\n- The point $(0,0)$ lies on the graph.\n\n- Its domain is $x\\in [0,\\infty).$\n\n- Its range is $y\\in [0,\\infty).$\n\n- The function is increasing over its entire domain.\n\n- As $x \\rightarrow \\infty,$ we have $y\\rightarrow \\infty,$ though the function increases more slowly as $x$ gets large.\n\nOf the given options, only graph II has all of the above properties.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is only II.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
